## rolling-energy

rolling-energy-ask = What share of the ball’s potential energy becomes kinetic, m·v²/2?
rolling-energy-title = A ball’s kinetic and potential energy
rolling-energy-question = We weigh a steel ball and let it go on a groove 5, 10 and 20 cm above the table. Having rolled down, it rolls along the table past two chalk marks 50 cm apart, and a phone films it in slow motion — 240 frames a second. From the frames we find the speed, work out the kinetic energy m·v²/2 and set it against the potential energy m·g·h the ball lost. What share of the ball’s potential energy becomes kinetic, m·v²/2?
rolling-energy-choices = All of it: energy is conserved — m·v²/2 = m·g·h
rolling-energy-choices-2 = Noticeably less than all — and the same share from any height
rolling-energy-choices-3 = Only a small share: friction eats almost all
rolling-energy-setup = A groove on a stand, a steel ball (25 mm), a laboratory balance, a ruler, two chalk marks on the table 50 cm apart, a phone filming in slow motion
